1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hernando_Pizarro
2 CONT
2 CONT Hernando Pizarro y de Vargas (born between 1478 and 1508, died 1578 or 1608) was a Spanish conquistador and one of the Pizarro brothers who ruled over Peru. He ultimately died in Spain of severely extended age, contrary to his bro
2 CONC thers who all suffered a violent fate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hernando was born in Trujillo, (Extremadura), Spain, son of Captain Gonzalo Pizarro y Rodríguez de Aguilar (senior) (1446-1522) - who as colonel of infantry served in the Italian campaigns under Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, and i
2 CONC n Navarre, with some distinction - and wife Isabel de Vargas.
2 CONT
2 CONT As one of the Pizarro brothers, he was related to Francisco, Juan, and Gonzalo Pizarro. He had two full sisters, Inés Pizarro y de Vargas, who married but did not have children with Francisco's only legitimate son, and Isabel Piza
2 CONC rro y de Vargas, married to Gonzalo de Tapia. Through his father, he was second cousins to Hernán Cortés.
2 CONT
2 CONT The New World Unlike his other brothers, his birth was legitimate, and he was educated and gained influence in the Spanish court. In 1530 Hernando departed for the New World with his half-brother, Francisco Pizarro, and accompanie
2 CONC d him during his conquests in Peru. In 1533 Hernando was sent back to advance the Pizarro interests against Francisco's uneasy partner, Diego de Almagro, as Hernando had the best connections in the Spanish court. When he returned 
2 CONC to Peru, he ruled with his other half-brothers (Juan and Gonzalo Pizarro) over the prized Inca capital of Cuzco. Governing with an iron fist, he helped with the eventual suppression of Inca uprisings led by Manco Capac.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Diego de Almagro returned from Chile on a fruitless gold-seeking expedition, he found that Hernando and his brothers were in control of Cuzco. However, as he had not obtained any credit for having been Francisco Pizarro's ma
2 CONC in partner in discovering Peru, he decided to claim Cuzco as part of his share. Almagro seized the city in 1537, capturing Hernando and Juan.  Hernando was eventually released after negotiations between Almagro and Francisco, and 
2 CONC in 1538 he and Gonzalo returned with an army to confront Almagro. In the ensuing Battle of Las Salinas, the Pizarros won a decisive victory, capturing Almagro and the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT The execution of Almagro later that year and the general disorder caused by the Spanish infighting caused substantial fallout in the Spanish court. Hernando was again called upon to leverage his royal contacts: in 1539 he returned
2 CONC  to Spain to lobby in favor of the Pizarros. Their perceived treachery was too great, however, and despite Hernando's bribery, he was imprisoned for the next twenty years. He left prison in 1560 and generally dropped out of sight,
2 CONC  reportedly dying at the age of 100 in the year 1608. Other sources suggest he died in 1578, and some that he was 72 at the time of death, which would make the year of his birth 1505 or 1506. If he in fact was 100 years old the ye
2 CONC ar of his birth would be 1478 or 1508.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family In 1552, Hernando married his niece, Francisca Pizarro Yupanqui (she was the daughter of Francisco Pizarro and his Inca mistress Inés Yupanqui) in Spain. Although born out of wedlock, she was legitimized by Imperial Decree)
2 CONC . They had five children. One of their sons, Francisco Pizarro y Pizarro, married twice and had offspring, the Marqueses de La Conquista. As a result, the Pizarro line survived Hernando's death, though currently it is extinct in m
2 CONC ale line.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was a son of Fernando Alonso Pizarro and wife Isabel de Vargas Rodríguez de Aguilar, paternal grandson of Fernando or Hernando Alonso de Hinojosa and wife Teresa Martínez Pizarro, and brother of Juan Pizarro, who died w
2 CONC ithout issue in 1521, and Diego Fernández Pizarro, who married Marina López and had a son Fernando Pizarro López, who had a natural son named Diego Pizarro de Vargas, married to Juana Rodríguez de Bobadilla, with female issue in P
2 CONC ortugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hernando Pizarro y de Vargas (1502, Trujillo (Extremadura), España-1578) fue un conquistador español.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hermano natural, y medio hermano por su padre, de Francisco Pizarro, Juan y Gonzalo, pasó al Perú con ellos y tomó parte de las primeras acciones de la conquista. También fue primo segundo de Hernán Cortés, conquistador de México.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nombrado gobernador del Cuzco, fue detenido por Almagro, pero Francisco Pizarro obtuvo su libertad. A partir de entonces fue uno de los que más fomentaron la rivalidad entre Francisco Pizarro y Almagro, al que mandó decapitar desp
2 CONC ués de la Batalla de las Salinas (1538). Ante las acusaciones de los amigos de Almagro contra Francisco Pizarro, éste envió a Hernando a España para defender su causa y logró el nombramiento de Cristóbal Vaca de Castro para poner 
2 CONC paz entre los dos partidos, pero la sospecha de estar implicado en la muerte de Alvarado, y la acusación por la muerte de Almagro, determinó su encarcelamiento en el Castillo de la Mota, en donde permaneció hasta 1560.
2 CONT
2 CONT Estando preso en el Castillo de la Mota (Medina del Campo/Valladolid), a la edad de 50 años, casó con su sobrina Francisca Pizarro Yupanqui (n.1534-d.1598), hija de Francisco Pizarro y de Inés Huaylas. Doña Francisca Pizarro Yupan
2 CONC qui, así como su hermano Gonzalo, fallecido en la infancia, habían sido nombrados herederos legítimos del Marqués de la conquista por Cédula Real dada en Monzón (Huesca/España) el 12 de octubre de 1537, por parte de Carlos I de Es
2 CONC paña y V de Alemania. A Doña. Francisca la había acompañado en su viaje a España su padrastro (segundo esposo de su madre, Inés Huaylas), Don Francisco de Ampuero, alcalde de Lima, y compañero de armas de Francisco Pizarro.
2 CONT --------------------
