1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hija natural de Sacerdote.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 1) Francisca González de la Nava, was a mestiza (mixed Spanish and Indian ancestry) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of the Captain Juan Sarmiento de Olivera (born c.1570 in Jérez de la Frontera, Andalucía, Spain).
2 CONT
2 CONT Francisca's grandfather (or great-grandfather?) was a conquistador, Bartolomé Hernández Herreño (1502-1558), who came from Spain to the New World at the age of 28 and reached Colombia at age 37 as part of the armada of Nikolaus Fe
2 CONC derman. There, Bartolomé discovered the "Río de Oro" (River of Gold) in the Santander region. He conquered and enslaved much of the region's indigenous population and he and his grown son died fighting an Indian rebellion. They we
2 CONC re hit by poisoned arrows and died in agony soon afterwards (for more details, read "Los compañeros de Féderman" by José Ignacio Avellaneda Navas). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father Francisco Sánchez Herreño, was a cleric who disregarded his vows and had a relationship with Beatriz de Torres, her mother a mestiza from Turmequé, an ancient Chibcha settlement. Their illegitimate daughter was Francisc
2 CONC a. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband, the Captain Juan Sarmiento de Olivera (born c.1570 in Jérez de la Frontera, Andalucía, Spain) was a young man when he came to the New World around 1590, and around 1600 he married Francisca González de la Nava in Véle
2 CONC z, Colombia. Vélez was settled in 1539 in the heart of Guane Indian territory, and Guanes inevitably bore some of the Sarmiento's descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Sarmiento and his wife Francisca had seven children (order unknown):
2 CONT
2 CONT i. Catalina Sarmiento de Olivera (b.1603 in Vélez), who married first Cristóbal de Rueda Rosales (b.1596) and then Captain Manuel Gómez Romano (see above).
2 CONT
2 CONT ii. María Antonia Sarmiento de Olivera (b.1605), who married Juan Díaz Bermúdez (b.1593 in Spain)
2 CONT
2 CONT iii. Margarita Sarmiento de Olivera (b.1607), who married Alonso de Rueda Rosales (b.1605), see above.
2 CONT
2 CONT iv. Alonso Sarmiento de Olivera, who married Cecilia de Cozar. 
2 CONT
2 CONT v. Juana Sarmiento de Olivera, who married Lázaro de Quiñónes Rincón.
2 CONT
2 CONT vi. Leonor Sarmiento de Olivera, who married the Spaniard Francisco Mantilla de los Rios y Palacios.
2 CONT
2 CONT vii. Fray Francisco Sarmiento de Olivera, a priest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From: 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://ruedafingerhut.blogspot.com/2009/07/origins-of-rueda-family-of-san-gil.html
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Esposa de Juan Sarmiento de Olivero. Se casan en 1600 en Vélez. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hija de Francisco Sánchez Herreño, clérigo y Beatriz de Torres, mestiza de Turmequé, nieta de Francisco Sánchez Herreño y Catalina Hernández, (su mujer) y biznieta de Bartolomé Hernández Herreño, descubridor del Río de Oro y de lo
2 CONC s primeros conquistadores del Nuevo Reino. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://espanol.oocities.com/sanjuangiron/textos/juan_sarmiento.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Esposa de Juan de Sarmiento. El capitán Juan Sarmiento, natural de Jerez de la Frontera, hijo de don Alonso de Olivera Sarmiento y de doña María de Guerra y Valderrama, pasó muy joven a América, por los años de 1590. Fue Alcald
2 CONC e ordinario y Teniente Gobernador de Girón, conquistador de los Yariguíes y uno de los primeros españoles en navegar por el río Sogamoso. Casó en 1600 en Vélez con doña Francisca González de la Nava, hija de Francisco Sánchez Herr
2 CONC eño, clérigo y Beatriz de Torres, mestiza de Turmequé, nieta de Francisco Sánchez Herreño y Catalina Hernández, su mujer y biznieta de Bartolomé Hernández Herreño, descubridor del Río de Oro y de los primeros conquistadores del Nu
2 CONC evo Reino. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://espanol.oocities.com/sanjuangiron/textos/juan_sarmiento.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Francisca Gonzalez de la Nava y Herreño
2 CONT 1580 – Fallecida	 • 	K8G6-P16
